  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cam Schlittler delivered six shutout innings and nothing mattered.",
    "what_this_means": "Arizona broke through once in the fifth when Schlittler's near-perfect outing ended with a run. The Yankees answered in the sixth, pushing ahead 3\u20132 on Austin Wells's second hit of the night. A moment they should have finished. Instead, the bullpen fractured. Michael Fulmer gave up two runs in the ninth, and a clean game became a loss. Six hits produced one run. RISP situations delivered once across six chances. They had the game. They didn't finish it.",
    "what_to_watch": "Schlittler was good enough to win. That he didn't reveals the real vulnerability. A starter's quality line is wasted when the bullpen breaks after you've given them a lead. The offense never built momentum beyond that sixth-inning push. They needed more, got nothing after, and ran out of chances. This works until the margin disappears. It's disappearing now. One run on six hits is not execution. It's a warning light.",
